The failure is in my "refreshTable re-reads external storage writes" test at line 484:

```cpp
f.session()->model_locations.SavePose(1, 0, Point6D(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6));
/*Without the refresh the model still serves the stale default.*/
REQUIRE(f.table()->data(f.table()->index(1, 0), PoseTableModel::XRole)
            .toDouble() == Approx(0.0));   // <-- FAILS: got 1.0
```

The table already re-reads the new value WITHOUT refreshTable() — why? Because `data()` calls `session_->model_locations.GetPose(index.row(), model_row_)` LIVE on every call! The PoseTableModel is not a cached snapshot — `data()` reads storage directly. So the table is ALWAYS fresh on read; the "stale" problem isn't the model's data() — it's that QML bindings don't re-EVALUATE until the model notifies (dataChanged/modelReset). The refresh() exists to trigger the notification (reset → QML re-reads).

So my test premise was wrong: direct data() reads are always live. The real observable of refreshTable() is the modelReset notification (QML re-evaluation). To pin the relay headlessly, I should spy on the model's rowsInserted/reset signal — e.g., QSignalSpy on modelReset, or connect to the model's `modelReset` signal and verify refreshTable() emits it.

Fix the test: 
```cpp
TEST_CASE("pose_bridge: refreshTable emits a model reset (the QML re-read trigger)") {
    PoseFixture f;
    QSignalSpy reset_spy(f.table(), &QAbstractItemModel::modelReset);
    f.poses()->refreshTable();
    REQUIRE(reset_spy.count() == 1);
}
```
QSignalSpy is a Qt Test header — the repo's headless tests use QSignalSpy? The lifecycle tests do. For unit tests, include <QSignalSpy> (QtTest). The target links Qt6::Core — QSignalSpy needs QtTest... QSignalSpy is in QtTest module. Hmm — does jtml_test_experimental_pose_bridge link Qt6::Test? Probably not (it's Catch2 + Qt6::Core). Adding QtTest linkage = CMake change (additive registration allowed — "additive test/CMakeLists.txt registrations"). Alternatively use a plain lambda connect to the modelReset signal with a counter — no QtTest needed:

```cpp
int resets = 0;
QObject::connect(f.table(), &QAbstractItemModel::modelReset,
                 [&resets]() { ++resets; });
f.poses()->refreshTable();
REQUIRE(resets == 1);
```

That's cleaner and needs no new linkage.

Also — the D3 drag test ("a viewer drag refreshes the table") — its assertion `data(index(0,0), XRole) == 42.0` passes for the WRONG reason (data() is live; it would pass without any refresh wiring). Hmm. To genuinely pin the wiring (viewerPoseApplied → refreshTable), I should assert the modelReset signal fired after applyViewerPose — that's the observable effect of the hub wiring:

```cpp
int resets = 0;
connect(table, &QAbstractItemModel::modelReset, [&]() { ++resets; });
f.study()->applyViewerPose(0, 42.0, ...);
REQUIRE(f.stored(0,0).x == Approx(42.0));
REQUIRE(resets == 1);   // the hub wiring refreshed the table
```

Wait — does applyViewerPose itself trigger any other model notification? applyViewerPose is on StudyBridge — it doesn't touch the table model. The only path is the hub wiring → refreshTable → modelReset. So resets == 1 pins the wiring precisely. 

But careful: fixture construction (PoseFixture) calls setCurrentFrame(0) + toggleModelSelected(0) → PoseBridge onSelectionChanged → setModelRow(0) → refresh() → modelReset fires during construction — but my counter connects AFTER the fixture is constructed (inside the TEST_CASE body), so it only counts post-connection resets. Good.

Let me rewrite both tests with the modelReset counter pattern.

Also check the "manual pose writes drop the pending ML seed" test — it passed (131 passed incl. all D4). Good.

Rewrite the two D3 tests.

